course: dictionary
lesson: 4261
title: Gratification: pleasure now or later
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Gratification is a naming word for a feeling. It is the good feeling you get when you get what you want, or when you finish something hard. «Waiting for something better later is called delayed gratification.» The word delayed is your first clue: the pleasure arrives later, after you have waited.
explain: Check the clues around the word. Waiting means not getting something straight away, so gratification must be the pleasure you feel when the waiting ends. Try it: swap in «the good feeling from waiting» for «delayed gratification». Does the sentence still make sense? If it does, you have the meaning.
explain: Gratification comes in two kinds. Instant gratification wants the pleasure right now. Delayed gratification means waiting for a bigger reward later. The words instant and delayed tell you which kind you are reading about, so use them as clues whenever you meet this word.
fact: Gratification means a feeling of pleasure or satisfaction when you get what you want.
fact: «Instant gratification» means wanting pleasure right now, without waiting.
fact: «Delayed gratification» means waiting for a bigger reward later.
fact: Gratification can also mean something that gives pleasure.
fact: In the famous marshmallow test, children could eat one sweet straight away or wait for a second sweet later.
exercise: «Waiting for something better later is called delayed gratification.» What does delayed gratification mean? | *Waiting for a bigger reward later | Getting pleasure right now | Feeling sleepy after lunch | Losing something you wanted
exercise: Which phrase means wanting pleasure right now, without waiting? | Patience for a long test | A sense of calm | Delayed gratification | *Instant gratification
exercise: Saving your pocket money for a bike next summer shows which idea? | Instant gratification | A sudden shock | A sense of boredom | *Delayed gratification
test: After finishing the tough puzzle, Mia felt a warm sense of gratification. What does this show? | Anger that the puzzle was hard | Hunger for lunch | *Pleasure and satisfaction at finishing | Sleepiness after a long day
test: Ben eats one sweet now instead of waiting for two later. Which idea is he choosing? | A slow reward | Delayed gratification | *Instant gratification | Gratitude for his friends
exam: In «delayed gratification», which word tells you the pleasure comes later? | Instant | Pleasure | *Delayed | Gratification
